PG.notes("s08-display", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the Advanced CSS (layout) section with the display property: block, inline, inline-block and none. A two-part exercise changes only display values to put three 200x200 squares in a row, then stack them vertically.</p>\n<h3>Why display matters</h3>\n<ul><li>A p element spans the whole width of the page (visible when its box is outlined with Pesticide), so the following content always starts on a new line.</li><li>display controls how an element takes part in the line flow. Changing it changes layout without changing the HTML.</li><li>The span element is the standard example of an inline element. It sits inside another element's text, for instance &lt;p&gt;Hello &lt;span&gt;Beautiful&lt;/span&gt; World&lt;/p&gt;, and does not break the line.</li></ul>\n<h3>The values</h3>\n<ul><li>display: block - the element takes the full available width, so neighbours go above or below it. Width and height can be set. Default for most elements (p, div, h1-h6, etc.).</li><li>display: inline - the element flows like text and shares a line with other inline items until the line is full, then wraps. Width and height are ignored because the size is just the content size. Default for span.</li><li>display: inline-block - a hybrid: the element sits on the same line as siblings (inline behaviour) but accepts width and height (block behaviour). Two boxes of different sizes will line up horizontally if the container is wide enough, and wrap if not.</li><li>display: none - the element is removed from the page entirely. Useful for hide/show buttons or when a to-do item is ticked off and should vanish.</li><li>There are many more display values; these are the ones met most often.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demo site</h3>\n<ul><li>The instructor points to an interactive demo with a block, an inline and an inline-block paragraph whose width and height can be adjusted. Observations: block accepts width/height but never lets a neighbour onto its line; inline never reacts to width/height; inline-block reacts and can share a line if narrow enough.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Between inline-block elements the whitespace in the HTML renders as a small gap; remember that if the boxes do not butt up against each other.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Span inside a paragraph</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;p&gt;Hello &lt;span&gt;Beautiful&lt;/span&gt; World&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Setting display in CSS</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.box { display: block; }         /* full row */\n.tag { display: inline; }        /* flows like text, no width/height */\n.tile { display: inline-block; width: 200px; height: 200px; }\n.gone { display: none; }         /* removed from layout */</pre>\n<h2>The task: Display property: row then column</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index1.html / index2.html: an h1 and three p elements (red, green, blue, white text) each with width and height 200px. The CSS sets different display values on them at the start (a mix of block and inline-type values), so they do not line up.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Using only the display property, (1) make the three squares sit in a horizontal row, all 200x200; (2) make them stack vertically, still 200x200.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Part 1: find the box whose display is inline - it ignores 200x200, so change it to inline-block; it becomes a square.</li><li>The box with display: block takes a whole row; change it to inline-block as well.</li><li>With all three inline-block, they line up in a row on a wide enough window and wrap on a narrow one.</li><li>Part 2: set all three to display: block. Each claims the full width, so they stack vertically.</li><li>Do not touch width, height or any other property.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Part 1: three equal squares side by side. Part 2: three equal squares in one column.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* Part 1: row */\n.red, .green, .blue { display: inline-block; width: 200px; height: 200px; }\n\n/* Part 2: column */\n.red, .green, .blue { display: block; width: 200px; height: 200px; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Setting width/height on an inline element and wondering why nothing changes.</li><li>Leaving one box as block, which forces a new line and breaks the row.</li><li>Using inline instead of inline-block when sizes are needed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The repo's index1/index2 are already solved versions and have an author footer; the original starting values differ.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s08-float", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains float (left/right) for wrapping text around an element, and clear for stopping later elements from wrapping. The exercise floats images, floats a cat block left and a dog block right, and clears the footer below both. Ends with advice to use floats only for text wrap and to prefer modern layout tools.</p>\n<h3>What float does</h3>\n<ul><li>float moves an element to the left or right edge of its container and lets following inline content (text) wrap around it. This mirrors print layout where text flows around a picture.</li><li>Values used in practice: left and right. Syntax: img { float: left; }.</li><li>In normal flow, an img and each p is block-level and takes a full row. A floated element is lifted out of that flow, so surrounding text wraps around it.</li><li>The property goes on the element that floats (the image), but the visible effect appears on the neighbouring text, which wraps.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stopping the wrap with clear</h3>\n<ul><li>If a footer follows floated content, it gets pulled up beside the float, which looks wrong; it should sit at the bottom at full width.</li><li>The fix is set on the footer, not the float: footer { clear: left | right | both }. It tells the element to drop below earlier floats on that side.</li><li>clear: left handles left floats, clear: right handles right floats, clear: both handles a layout that floats elements on both sides.</li><li>Typical case: two floated divs (one left, one right) with a footer needing clear: both.</li></ul>\n<h3>When to use it</h3>\n<ul><li>Floats used to be stretched into complex page layouts. That is now discouraged because results can be unpredictable.</li><li>Use float mainly to wrap text around an image. For layouts, use Flexbox, Grid or frameworks such as Bootstrap (covered later).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Floated children do not give their parent any height, a classic reason a container collapses; clear or modern layout avoids this.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Text wrapping around an image</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">img { float: left; }   /* or right */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Footer cleared of floats</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">footer { clear: both; }</pre>\n<h2>The task: Cats and dogs float layout</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with a div.cat (aquamarine background, h2, img, p) and a div.dog (coral), each at 40% width and display: inline-block, followed by a footer. The two blocks sit side by side and text does not wrap around the images.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Match goal.png: text wraps around each image, the cat block hugs the left, the dog block hugs the right, and the footer spans below both.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add img { float: left; } so each paragraph wraps around its image (needs enough window width).</li><li>Add float: left to .cat and float: right to .dog.</li><li>The footer jumps up between the blocks; add clear: both to footer so it returns to the bottom.</li><li>Slightly different block heights (different text lengths) are expected, not a bug.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Two coloured columns at the page edges with wrapped text and a full-width footer underneath.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">div { display: inline-block; width: 40%; }\np { font-size: 2em; }\n.cat { float: left;  background-color: aquamarine; }\n.dog { float: right; background-color: coral; }\nimg  { float: left; }\nfooter { clear: both; text-align: center; background-color: blueviolet; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting clear on the floated element instead of the one that must drop below.</li><li>Footer rising between floated blocks because clear was omitted.</li><li>Using float as a general layout system.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Repo files include the author footer ('Made with...') which is not part of the exercise.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s08-responsive", "<p class=\"summary\">Overview of the four main ways to make a page respond to screen width: media queries, CSS Grid, Flexbox and the Bootstrap framework. Each gets a short demo; Grid, Flexbox and Bootstrap are previewed ahead of their own sections, and the lesson ends by suggesting you experiment with the provided demo files.</p>\n<h3>What 'responsive' means</h3>\n<ul><li>Pages must look right on desktops, laptops, tablets and phones, so the layout has to adapt to the viewport width.</li><li>Try resizing a real site's window: navigation items collapse into a menu at some width, and a search box may vanish on narrow screens. Those switch-over widths are breakpoints.</li><li>Four techniques: media queries, CSS Grid, Flexbox, and an external framework such as Bootstrap. There is no single best one; they are tools with different strengths.</li></ul>\n<h3>1. Media queries</h3>\n<ul><li>Written where a selector would go, starting with @media followed by a condition in parentheses, then a block of normal rules.</li><li>@media (max-width: 600px) { ... } applies the inner rules whenever the viewport is 600px wide or narrower.</li><li>Different widths and different rule sets can be defined, one block per breakpoint. Detailed in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. CSS Grid (2D layout)</h3>\n<ul><li>Put display: grid on a container; its direct children become grid items.</li><li>grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr defines two equal columns. fr is a fraction of the available space.</li><li>grid-template-rows: 100px 200px 200px defines three rows with fixed heights (rows are often given in px).</li><li>gap: 30px sets spacing between rows and columns.</li><li>grid-column: span 2 on one item (class .first) makes it cover both columns, producing a wide header-like cell.</li><li>Example uses a container div with five .card children. Described as flexible for complex layouts with only a few lines. A whole section follows.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. Flexbox (1D layout)</h3>\n<ul><li>Flexbox lays items along one axis, either a row or a column. Put display: flex on the container.</li><li>flex: 1 on each child gives all children equal share of the width. flex: 2 makes a child twice as wide as a flex: 1 sibling; flex: 0.5 makes it half as wide.</li><li>Sizes are ratios, not pixels, so they scale with the container automatically. In a vertical flexbox the same ratios divide the height (for example header 1, main 3, footer 1 = 5 parts).</li><li>Demo has four cards with a fixed height of 100px; the first gets flex 2, the second flex 0.5.</li></ul>\n<h3>4. Bootstrap framework</h3>\n<ul><li>A framework is external CSS (and JS) brought into the page; it defines many ready-made class names such as card, row, container.</li><li>Loaded through a link tag to its stylesheet CDN; adding class names applies rounded corners, fonts and layout without writing the CSS.</li><li>Its grid is built on Flexbox and divides the width into 12 columns. col-6 spans 6 of 12 (50%), col-2 spans 2, col-4 spans 4; 6+2+4 = 12 fills the row.</li><li>Widths are percentages, so col-6 is 500px in a 1000px container and 1000px in 2000px.</li></ul>\n<h3>Choosing a method</h3>\n<ul><li>Because each technique is suited to different jobs, learn all of them and mix as needed; later sections cover Grid, Flexbox and Bootstrap in depth.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In real projects they are combined, for example Flexbox for a nav bar, Grid for the page, media queries for tweaks at breakpoints.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Media query breakpoint</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">div { background-color: rebeccapurple; height: 500px; width: 500px; }\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  div { height: 200px; width: 200px; }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">CSS Grid container with a spanning item</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.grid-container {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;\n  grid-template-rows: 100px 200px 200px;\n  gap: 30px;\n}\n.first { grid-column: span 2; }\n.card { background-color: blue; }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Flexbox container with equal children</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.flex-container { display: flex; }\n.card { background: blue; border: 30px solid white; height: 100px; flex: 1; }\n/* in the demo: .first { flex: 2 }  .second { flex: 0.5 } */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Bootstrap 12-column row</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;link href=&quot;https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.0.2/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css&quot; rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;row&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;card col-6&quot;&gt;Card&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;card col-2&quot;&gt;Card&lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;card col-4&quot;&gt;Card&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Explore the four responsiveness demos</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download '8.2 Responsiveness'. index.html links to media-query.html, grid.html, flexbox.html and bootstrap.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Open each demo, resize the preview and tweak values to see how each technique reacts. No fixed solution.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open index.html in the preview and follow the links.</li><li>In media-query.html change the 600px breakpoint and the sizes.</li><li>In grid.html, change the column fractions, row heights, gap and the span.</li><li>In flexbox.html, change flex values on .first and .second.</li><li>In bootstrap.html, change the col-N numbers (keep a row to 12 total) and resize the window.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Familiarity with how each technique rescales the layout; nothing to submit.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Expecting a single 'best' technique.</li><li>Using fixed px widths for everything, so layouts cannot adapt.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Forgetting the viewport meta tag, which stops media queries behaving properly on phones.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Repo demo files carry an author footer; ignore it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s08-media-query", "<p class=\"summary\">Detailed look at @media rules: max-width, min-width, combining both with and, plus the screen/print media types. The exercise builds four breakpoints that change the body background colour for mobile, tablet, laptop and desktop widths.</p>\n<h3>Anatomy of a media query</h3>\n<ul><li>Written in place of a selector: @media (condition) { normal rules }. The rules inside only apply when the condition is true, and they override the default styles outside the block.</li><li>@media (max-width: 600px) means 'viewport at most 600px wide'. Typical use: shrink an h1, or reduce a 200x200 blue div to 100x100 on phones.</li><li>@media (min-width: 600px) flips direction: it targets viewports 600px and wider, i.e. the bigger-screen end.</li><li>Think of the width axis from 0 to roughly 2400px: max-width styles the left portion up to the breakpoint, min-width styles everything to the right.</li></ul>\n<h3>Combining conditions</h3>\n<ul><li>Join conditions with the keyword and: @media (min-width: 600px) and (max-width: 900px) targets only widths in that band.</li><li>The same pattern can express 'outside a band' by reversing which side each limit is on (small max-width plus separate larger min-width rule). Read each limit literally: max-width is the highest width where the rules apply, min-width the lowest.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Boundaries are inclusive, so adjacent bands should differ by 1px (480 / 481) to avoid overlap.</li></ul>\n<h3>Media types and other features</h3>\n<ul><li>You may see screen, as in @media screen and (max-width: 600px). Screen is effectively the default so it is rarely needed; the other common type is print, which applies rules only when the page is printed.</li><li>MDN's 'Using media queries' page lists more features: orientation, height, width, device-width and device-height, and media types. Most real work only needs min-width and max-width.</li></ul>\n<h3>Testing in Chrome DevTools</h3>\n<ul><li>Open DevTools (Chrome menu, More tools, Developer tools) and toggle the device toolbar.</li><li>Choose a preset (iPhone SE, iPhone 12, iPad Air) or Responsive and drag or type an exact width to check which breakpoint triggers.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">max-width, min-width and both</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">div { width: 200px; height: 200px; background-color: blue; }\n\n@media (max-width: 600px) {          /* phones and narrower */\n  div { width: 100px; height: 100px; }\n}\n@media (min-width: 600px) { /* wider screens */ }\n@media (min-width: 600px) and (max-width: 900px) { /* only in between */ }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Print only</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">@media print { nav { display: none; } }</pre>\n<h2>The task: Device-width background colours</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with body background aquamarine and TODO comments listing four ranges: Mobile 319-480px, tablets 481-1200px, laptops 1201-1600px, desktops 1601px and up. An empty body.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the body background change by width, matching solution.html: lightsalmon (mobile), powderblue (tablet), limegreen (laptop), seagreen (desktop).</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add @media (min-width: 319px) and (max-width: 480px) with body { background-color: lightsalmon } (max-width alone also works since few screens are narrower than 320px).</li><li>Add @media (min-width: 481px) and (max-width: 1200px) with powderblue.</li><li>Add @media (min-width: 1201px) and (max-width: 1600px) with limegreen.</li><li>Add @media (min-width: 1601px) with seagreen; no max needed.</li><li>Test by resizing the browser or the DevTools device toolbar.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Resizing the window flips the page between four colours at 480/481, 1200/1201 and 1600/1601px; wider than 1601 is seagreen. Below 319px the default aquamarine shows.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body { background-color: aquamarine; }\n@media (min-width: 319px) and (max-width: 480px)  { body { background-color: lightsalmon; } }\n@media (min-width: 481px) and (max-width: 1200px) { body { background-color: powderblue; } }\n@media (min-width: 1201px) and (max-width: 1600px) { body { background-color: limegreen; } }\n@media (min-width: 1601px) { body { background-color: seagreen; } }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mixing up max and min direction (max-width styles smaller screens).</li><li>Overlapping or gapping ranges, such as 480 to 482.</li><li>Forgetting the viewport meta tag, so phones report a wide viewport.</li><li>Omitting the parentheses around each condition.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s08-agency", "<p class=\"summary\">Section 8 capstone: take a plain starter page for a design agency and use float, display, inline/block behaviour and a media query to produce a two-card layout that stacks on phones. The walkthrough afterwards highlights three details: a span for partial colouring, the 680px breakpoint, and object-fit.</p>\n<h3>What the finished page looks like</h3>\n<ul><li>Header area with a logo, then an h1 along the lines of 'We are a Creative Design Agency', then two side-by-side cards (image + title + paragraph each), then a footer with the company motto.</li><li>On wide screens the left card floats left and the right card floats right, each about 45% wide, so they form two columns.</li><li>On narrow screens (roughly phone width) the cards stop being columns and stack vertically, each spanning the full width, with centred heading and justified text.</li></ul>\n<h3>Skills the project is meant to exercise</h3>\n<ul><li>Block vs inline vs inline-block behaviour (the display property).</li><li>float: left / float: right (and clear for the footer) to build columns and wrap text around images.</li><li>Responsiveness through a viewport meta tag and a max-width media query.</li><li>The brief explicitly says an identical copy is not required: any attractive layout that uses these tools is acceptable; breaking the problem down yourself is the real goal.</li></ul>\n<h3>Working with the starter files</h3>\n<ul><li>Starter folder contains index.html (the file to edit, already has fonts, images and text but no layout), solution.html and its stylesheet, plus an assets/images folder (logo, two card images).</li><li>Open both index.html and solution.html in Live Preview side by side; copy the preview URL into a real browser to resize the window and test the breakpoint.</li><li>Recommended order: attempt it yourself first, only then read the solution. Share especially good variations in the course Q&amp;A.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough: key details</h3>\n<ul><li>The word 'Creative' is wrapped in &lt;span class=&quot;creative&quot;&gt; so only that word gets its own colour (midnightblue) while staying in the normal text flow; span is an inline element so it does not break the line.</li><li>Cards use two layout classes plus a shared class: class=&quot;left card&quot; and class=&quot;right card&quot;. .card sets width: 45%; .left floats left; .right floats right.</li><li>Card images use a .tile-image class with a fixed height (200px), float: left and a right margin, so the title and paragraph wrap around the image.</li><li>A media query for max-width: 680px targets phones and small tablets. Inside it: .card gets display: block, width: 100%, a bottom margin and text-align: justify; the card image gets width: 100%, display: inline and object-fit: cover; the h1 shrinks and is centred; the logo is made smaller.</li><li>text-align: justify stretches each line to both edges so the paragraph looks like a clean block on a narrow screen.</li><li>object-fit is new in this lesson: it controls how an image fills a box whose size differs from the image's aspect ratio (cover crops to fill without distortion). Advice given: when you meet an unfamiliar property, look it up on MDN; that is normal professional workflow.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference CSS also makes body a flex column with min-height: 95vh and gives .main flex: 1 so the footer sticks to the bottom of short pages; that is a flexbox trick from the next section and is not explained here.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The starter footer needs to sit below the floated cards; in the reference this works because .main is a flex item that contains the floats, otherwise clear: both on the footer is the classic fix.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core layout CSS from the reference solution (condensed)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.tile-image { height: 200px; float: left; margin-right: 50px; }\n.card  { width: 45%; }\n.left  { float: left; }\n.right { float: right; }\n.creative { color: midnightblue; }\nfooter { text-align: right; color: midnightblue; }\n\n@media (max-width: 680px) {\n  .logo { width: 100px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 3.5rem; text-align: center; }\n  .card { width: 100%; display: block; margin-bottom: 30px; text-align: justify; }\n  .card img { margin-bottom: 10px; width: 100%; display: inline; object-fit: cover; }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Heading with a coloured word (span)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;We are a &lt;span class=&quot;creative&quot;&gt;Creative&lt;/span&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Design Agency&lt;/h1&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Section 8 project: Web Design Agency Website</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter index.html with fonts, logo, two card images, headings, paragraphs and a footer already in place, but with no layout CSS; a solution.html is included for comparison.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Style the page into a two-column card layout using float/display and make it responsive so the cards stack on narrow screens.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open index.html and solution.html in Live Preview and compare; open the preview URL in a full browser so the window can be resized.</li><li>Wrap the word 'Creative' in a span with a class and colour it in the stylesheet.</li><li>Give both cards a width (about 45%), float one left and one right so they sit side by side.</li><li>Float the card images left with a right margin so the text wraps around them.</li><li>Add a max-width media query (around 600-680px) that gives the cards full width and display: block so they stack, and adjust image, heading and text alignment for small screens.</li><li>Resize the browser to check the breakpoint, then compare with solution.html and look up unfamiliar properties such as object-fit on MDN.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Wide screen: two floated cards side by side below the heading, footer at the bottom. Narrow screen: cards stacked full width with justified text and a centred heading.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body { font-family: &quot;Poppins&quot;, sans-serif; margin: 50px 50px 0 50px; background-color: #faf9f6; display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 95vh; }\n.main { flex: 1; }\nh1 { font-size: 5rem; }\nfooter { text-align: right; color: midnightblue; }\n.tile-image { height: 200px; float: left; margin-right: 50px; }\n.card { width: 45%; }\n.left { float: left; }\n.right { float: right; }\n.creative { color: midnightblue; }\n@media (max-width: 680px) {\n  .logo { width: 100px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 3.5rem; text-align: center; }\n  .card { width: 100%; display: block; margin-bottom: 30px; text-align: justify; }\n  .card img { margin-bottom: 10px; width: 100%; display: inline; object-fit: cover; }\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Floated cards collapse their parent's height, so the footer can ride up unless the parent contains the floats or the footer uses clear: both.</li><li>Forgetting the viewport meta tag, which stops media queries behaving as expected on phones.</li><li>Peeking at the solution before attempting the layout.</li><li>The reference repo's solution.html has an author footer ('Made with love by ...') that is not part of the course.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l053", "<p class=\"summary\">Short study-habit tip: avoid ritual preparation before working, start immediately, and reward yourself afterwards. Suggests the Pomodoro Technique.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Elaborate setup rituals (tidying the desk, snacks, tea) are themselves a form of delay and reward the avoidance habit.</li><li>Treat procrastination like a habit that strengthens when fed: start the work first, take the treat afterwards.</li><li>Pomodoro Technique: work for 25 minutes with a timer, then take a 5-minute break. Keep small distractions for the break so both work and rest get full attention.</li><li>Share what works for you in the course Q&amp;A.</li></ul>");
